{"$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json", "version": "2.1.0", "runs": [{"tool": {"driver": {"name": "code-forge", "semanticVersion": "code-forge 2.7.0 [ruff=ruff 0.15.13 shellcheck=ShellCheck - shell script analysis tool]", "informationUri": "https://github.com/HouMinXi/code-forge"}}, "results": [{"ruleId": "7775b92efadf8ee8", "level": "error", "message": {"text": "[expert] Misleading comment: 'each of these two fields' refers only to 'code_excerpts' (the sole entry in _NESTED_SCHEMAS) while 'covered_line_ranges' is deliberately ignored. The comment should say 'this field' to avoid confusion."}, "locations": [{"physicalLocation": {"artifactLocation": {"uri": "src/code_forge/verify.py"}, "region": {"startLine": 113, "endLine": 113}}}], "properties": {"source": "L1"}}, {"ruleId": "a7a4cab6de1860a1", "level": "warning", "message": {"text": "[adversarial] Coverage inflation via oversized excerpt ranges: `_cycle_excerpt_covered` uses the full `start_line`\u2013`end_line` range of each code excerpt without verifying that the excerpt content actually contains those lines. A malicious receipt can claim a large range (e.g., 1\u20131000) while providing only a few matching lines, inflating the coverage denominator and passing the 60% threshold. STEP C only compares lines that overlap between the excerpt and the diff post-image; it does not require the excerpt content to cover its declared range. Fix: compare the number of lines in `content` to `end_line - start_line + 1` and reject mismatches, or compute coverage from the actual lines present in `content`."}, "locations": [{"physicalLocation": {"artifactLocation": {"uri": "src/code_forge/verify.py"}, "region": {"startLine": 326, "endLine": 326}}}], "properties": {"source": "L1"}}, {"ruleId": "f6b5eb0132e21481", "level": "error", "message": {"text": "[adversarial] The comment in `_validate_receipt_schema` states that `covered_line_ranges` is deliberately not checked, yet the legacy `_covered()` function accesses `r[\"start\"]` and `r[\"end\"]` assuming a dict shape. If a receipt with string-format `covered_line_ranges` is loaded (even though production never takes the legacy branch), `_covered()` will crash with a `TypeError` (string indices must be integers). While the legacy branch is not used in production, this is a latent crash that could surface if `hardened=False` is ever exercised with such receipts. Either add a guard in `_covered()` or move the comment into the function and add a defensive check."}, "locations": [{"physicalLocation": {"artifactLocation": {"uri": "src/code_forge/verify.py"}, "region": {"startLine": 147, "endLine": 147}}}], "properties": {"source": "L1"}}], "properties": {"tokenCost": {"inputTokens": 60723, "outputTokens": 35680, "totalTokens": 96403, "backend": "deepseek", "model": "deepseek-v4-flash", "passes": 3, "durationSeconds": 91.4}, "advisories": [{"id": "runtime-0", "axis": "RUNTIME", "file": "src/code_forge/verify.py", "lineRange": [66, 66], "description": "Partially written receipt file (e.g., from concurrent receipt writer) causes JSON decode error, reported as corrupt receipt. May cause transient false failures. No file locking is used.", "attribution": "runtime-axis/llm"}, {"id": "runtime-1", "axis": "RUNTIME", "file": "src/code_forge/verify.py", "lineRange": [66, 66], "description": "If a receipt file is a directory (e.g., someone created a directory named 'receipt-c*.json'), f.read_text() raises IsADirectoryError (subclass of OSError). Caught and reported, but error message correctly names the file.", "attribution": "runtime-axis/llm"}, {"id": "runtime-2", "axis": "RUNTIME", "file": "src/code_forge/verify.py", "lineRange": [66, 66], "description": "Extremely large receipt file (e.g., deeply nested JSON) could cause MemoryError. MemoryError is intentionally not caught, causing an unhandled exception that may crash the caller.", "attribution": "runtime-axis/llm"}, {"id": "runtime-3", "axis": "RUNTIME", "file": "src/code_forge/ver